::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Perspective: Weekly planning helps students manage long-term assignments. Which slide note is clearest? {
~A. Planning has a lot of stuff that happens.#Incorrect. This wording is vague and does not help the audience understand the point.
=B. Weekly planning breaks long assignments into manageable steps.#Correct. This note is concise and clearly supports the presentation perspective.
~C. Assignments are things students do.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not support the perspective.
~D. There are many weeks in a school year.#Incorrect. This fact does not explain how weekly planning helps.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which source is most credible for a slide about online source evaluation? {
~A. An anonymous post with no author or date#Incorrect. Missing source information makes the evidence weak for an academic presentation.
~B. A meme about copying homework#Incorrect. A meme is not credible evidence for this presentation purpose.
~C. A friend's unsupported opinion about websites#Incorrect. An unsupported opinion does not provide reliable research evidence.
=D. A school research guide explaining how to check author, date, and purpose#Correct. This source is relevant, reliable, and connected to source evaluation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Weak slide note: Feedback can help with lots of writing stuff before things get turned in. Which revision is clearest? {
=A. Feedback helps writers clarify ideas before final submission.#Correct. This revision is concise and clearly explains the idea.
~B. Feedback and writing things are pretty helpful.#Incorrect. This remains vague and informal. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Writing stuff changes sometimes.#Incorrect. This does not explain how feedback helps.
~D. Feedback happens when people say things.#Incorrect. This is too broad and does not support the presentation perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L05 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which phrase uses academic vocabulary best on a slide about evidence? {
~A. source stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is vague and informal.
~B. things from somewhere#Incorrect. This wording does not identify evidence precisely.
=C. credible evidence from reliable sources#Correct. This phrase is precise and appropriate for a digital presentation.
~D. facts and stuff#Incorrect. This is informal and too broad. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
A slide contains a full paragraph copied from a source. What is the best revision? {
~A. Add a second paragraph so the slide looks complete.#Incorrect. More text would overload the audience.
~B. Remove all source information from the presentation.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed, but it should be presented clearly.
=C. Replace the paragraph with a concise evidence note and explain the details orally.#Correct. This keeps the slide readable while preserving the evidence.
~D. Shrink the font until all the text fits.#Incorrect. Smaller text does not improve clarity or audience awareness.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which evidence best supports a presentation about source credibility? {
=A. A source-checking checklist from the school media center#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. A random picture of a computer#Incorrect. A picture alone does not provide evidence about credibility.
~C. A rumor that websites are usually fine#Incorrect. A rumor is not reliable evidence. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. A list of unrelated website colors#Incorrect. Website colors do not prove source credibility.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which slide is most audience-aware for ninth-grade students beginning research? {
~A. "Epistemological source triangulation protocols"#Incorrect. This wording is too advanced and may confuse the intended audience.
~B. "Sources are sources, so just use them."#Incorrect. This is inaccurate and not audience-aware.
~C. "Research is impossible to understand."#Incorrect. This discourages the audience and does not teach a clear strategy.
=D. "Check who wrote the source, when it was published, and why it was created."#Correct. This wording is clear and useful for ninth-grade students.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which revision improves slide cohesion? {
~A. Put unrelated evidence on the same slide.#Incorrect. Unrelated evidence weakens cohesion.
=B. Use a slide title that connects the evidence to the presentation perspective.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Add random facts from several topics.#Incorrect. Random facts weaken cohesion. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. Remove the connection between evidence and claim.#Incorrect. The audience needs that connection.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L05 Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which word best completes the slide note? "Use _____ evidence to support digital presentation claims." {
=A. credible#Correct. Credible evidence is trustworthy and appropriate for support.
~B. decorative#Incorrect. Decoration is not the same as evidence.
~C. random#Incorrect. Random evidence would not provide trustworthy support.
~D. confusing#Incorrect. Confusing evidence would weaken audience understanding.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which slide note is too vague? {
~A. Source checks help students avoid outdated information.#Incorrect. This is clear and specific. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Written due dates help students track long-term assignments.#Incorrect. This is specific and understandable.
~C. Feedback can reveal unclear evidence before final submission.#Incorrect. This clearly supports a presentation point.
=D. Stuff helps people do things better.#Correct. This note is too vague to support a digital presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
A student finds evidence but cannot identify the author, date, or source. What should the student do? {
~A. Put it on a slide without explanation.#Incorrect. Unverified evidence weakens credibility.
~B. Use it because all online information is reliable.#Incorrect. Online information must be evaluated.
=C. Avoid using it unless the source can be verified.#Correct. Evidence should be reliable and traceable before it supports a presentation.
~D. Use it only if it is colorful.#Incorrect. Visual appeal does not prove reliability.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which slide revision improves clarity? {
~A. Replace specific wording with unclear abbreviations.#Incorrect. Unclear abbreviations can confuse the audience.
=B. Change "evaluation of source reliability" to "checking whether a source can be trusted."#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Change "clear evidence" to "stuff."#Incorrect. This makes the wording less precise.
~D. Add three unrelated examples to the same slide.#Incorrect. This would make the slide less clear.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which choice best explains how evidence should function on a slide? {
~A. It should decorate the slide even if it does not support the claim.#Incorrect. Evidence is not decoration. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. It should replace the speaker's entire explanation.#Incorrect. Slides should support, not replace, speaking.
=C. It should support the perspective and give the speaker something meaningful to explain.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. It should be unrelated so the audience is surprised.#Incorrect. Unrelated evidence breaks coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L05 Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which revision uses stronger academic vocabulary? {
~A. "good source stuff" instead of "reliable evidence"#Incorrect. This revision weakens precision. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. "things that seem okay" instead of "credible sources"#Incorrect. This is vague and informal. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. "random website facts" instead of "research findings"#Incorrect. This weakens the academic tone. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=D. "reliable evidence" instead of "good source stuff"#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Weak slide title: "A Lot of Things About Feedback." Which revision is best? {
=A. Why Feedback Improves Final Drafts#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Feedback and stuff#Incorrect. This is still vague and informal. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Many things happen#Incorrect. This does not identify the slide's focus.
~D. Words on a slide#Incorrect. This is not a meaningful title. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which slide note best supports a presentation for school leaders? {
~A. Some papers are long.#Incorrect. This does not support a clear presentation perspective.
~B. Due dates are annoying to lots of people.#Incorrect. This is informal and unsupported. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Everybody knows reminders are good.#Incorrect. This assumes support instead of providing evidence.
=D. Student survey results show that written reminders reduce missed due dates.#Correct. This evidence is specific, credible, and appropriate for school leaders.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which question helps evaluate source reliability? {
~A. Does the source have many colors?#Incorrect. Color does not determine credibility.
=B. Who created the source and why was it created?#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Is the source title written in a large font?#Incorrect. Font size does not prove reliability.
~D. Can the source be copied quickly?#Incorrect. Ease of copying is not a reliability measure.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which feedback would best help revise a slide? {
~A. "Add more random colors."#Incorrect. Color alone does not improve evidence or clarity.
~B. "Use every fact you found."#Incorrect. Too many facts can overload a slide.
=C. "Make the evidence connection clearer for the audience."#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. "Remove the main perspective."#Incorrect. The perspective is needed for coherence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which slide text is most concise and clear? {
=A. Check sources before using evidence.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. The thing that we are trying to say is that sources should be looked at carefully before using them.#Incorrect. This is wordy for a slide. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Sources and evidence and things and looking.#Incorrect. This is unclear and incomplete. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. Research maybe needs stuff checked sometimes.#Incorrect. This is vague and uncertain. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L05 Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which academic word best replaces "good enough to trust"? {
~A. crowded#Incorrect. Crowded describes too much content, not reliability.
=B. credible#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. colorful#Incorrect. Colorful does not mean trustworthy.
~D. random#Incorrect. Random information is not necessarily trustworthy.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
A slide note says, "Student survey: written reminders reduce missed deadlines." What should the speaker do orally? {
~A. Read the note and immediately stop speaking.#Incorrect. The audience needs explanation. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Ignore the slide and discuss an unrelated topic.#Incorrect. The oral explanation should connect to the slide.
~C. Add a full paragraph to the slide while speaking.#Incorrect. The slide should stay concise. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=D. Explain how the survey supports the presentation's perspective.#Correct. The speaker should connect the concise slide note to the larger idea.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.RS.01
Which revision best improves audience awareness? {
=A. Choose examples and vocabulary the intended audience can understand.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Replace clear student-friendly wording with technical jargon.#Incorrect. Jargon can confuse the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Remove all explanations from the slides and speech.#Incorrect. The audience still needs support. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. Add unrelated facts to impress the audience.#Incorrect. Unrelated facts weaken clarity. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L05 Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which evidence should be avoided? {
~A. A dated school survey with clear results#Incorrect. This is likely usable evidence. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. A teacher observation connected to the topic#Incorrect. This can be relevant evidence. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=C. An online post with no author, source, or support#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. A research guide from the media center#Incorrect. This is a credible source for research guidance.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L05 Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.W.04
Which slide plan best balances evidence and clarity? {
~A. Several unrelated facts in small font#Incorrect. This weakens clarity and coherence.
=B. One concise evidence note plus oral explanation#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Five copied paragraphs on one slide#Incorrect. This would overload the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. No evidence and only a decorative image#Incorrect. The presentation still needs support.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.04 U06 L05 Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
A slide is accurate but hard to understand. What should the student do? {
~A. Leave it unchanged because accuracy is the only concern.#Incorrect. Digital presentations also need clarity and audience awareness.
~B. Remove the evidence and use decoration instead.#Incorrect. Evidence should remain if it supports the perspective.
~C. Add more complex words to make it sound harder.#Incorrect. More complex wording may reduce audience understanding.
=D. Revise the wording so the accurate idea is easier for the audience to follow.#Correct. Revision should preserve accuracy while improving clarity.
}
